  "answer_text": "Here's a breakdown of typical linebacker jersey numbers in the NFL:\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udccb Current NFL Linebacker Number Rules (Post-2021 Expansion)\n\nLinebackers are currently permitted to wear numbers **1\u201359** and **90\u201399**.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udd22 Most Common/Traditional Ranges\n\n- **50s (50\u201359):** Numbers 50 to 59 are traditionally worn by linebackers and centers. This has historically been the most iconic range for linebackers.\n\n- **90s (90\u201399):** Numbers 90 to 99 are worn by linebackers and defensive linemen.\n\n- **40s (40\u201349):** The NFL Competition Committee made a change in 2015, allowing linebackers to use numbers 40 through 49 for the first time.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udcdc History of Linebacker Numbering\n\n- In 1984, the NFL allowed linebackers to wear jersey numbers in the 90\u201399 range, since more teams were making use of the 3\u20134 defense and were quickly exhausting numbers for linebackers, who previously were only allowed to wear numbers in the 50\u201359 range.\n\n- In 2021, NFL owners passed a rule expanding available numbers, with linebackers permitted to wear **1\u201379 and 90\u201399**. (The current official rule settled at **1\u201359 and 90\u201399** per NFL Football Operations.)\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udccc Summary Table\n\n| Number Range | Notes |\n|---|---|\n| **1\u201359** | Allowed (expanded in 2021) |\n| **90\u201399** | Traditional for pass-rushing LBs |\n| **50\u201359** | Classic, most iconic LB range |\n| **40\u201349** | Allowed since 2015 |\n| **60\u201379** | \u274c Not permitted for LBs |\n| **80\u201389** | \u274c Not permitted for LBs |\n\nThe most \"classic\" linebacker numbers remain in the **50s** and **90s**, though today's players have much more flexibility with the expanded rules.",
